28, 30.174, 30.221, 30.244, 30.302, 30.348, 30.395, 30.43, 30.488, 30.557, 30.604, 30.65, 30.697, 30.743, 30.778, 30.801, 30.871, 30.917, 30.975, 31.01, 31.068, 31.115, 31.196, 31.254, 31.335, 31.393, 31.451, 31.521, 31.579, 31.625, 31.672, 31.73, 31.846, 31.916, 32.032, 32.09, 32.206, 32.264, 32.31, 32.345, 32.392, 32.485, 32.508, 32.554, 32.647, 32.74, 32.81, 32.926, 33.042, 33.088, 33.123, 33.193, 33.239, 33.297, 33.344, 33.39, 33.425, 33.46, 33.495, 33.541, 33.576, 33.692, 33.773, 33.843, 33.913, 33.994, 34.191, 34.272, 34.331, 34.377, 34.412, 34.435, 34.458, 34.516, 34.563, 34.621, 34.679, 34.725, 34.772, 34.807, 34.83, 34.888, 34.934, 35.016, 35.062, 35.108, 35.224, 35.341, 35.828, 35.886, 35.956, 36.037, 36.084, 36.142, 36.177, 36.223, 36.258, 36.304, 36.339, 36.397, 36.444, 36.49, 36.525, 36.548, 36.594, 36.664, 36.711, 36.78, 36.827, 36.896, 36.954, 37.001, 37.036, 37.105, 37.152, 37.21, 37.268, 37.314, 37.372, 37.43, 37.488, 37.546, 37.593, 37.616, 37.639, 37.663, 37.686, 37.744, 37.779, 37.837, 37.883, 37.999, 38.115, 38.255, 38.371, 38.44, 38.487, 38.522, 38.568, 38.615, 38.661, 38.707, 38.754, 38.8, 38.824, 38.882, 38.916, 38.975, 39.021, 39.079, 39.102, 39.148, 39.172, 39.206, 39.288, 39.381, 39.45, 39.52, 39.59, 39.648, 39.682, 39.717, 39.787, 39.88, 39.95, 40.008, 40.054, 40.124, 40.182, 40.205, 40.24, 40.309, 40.391, 40.46, 40.53, 40.588, 40.658, 40.704, 40.774, 40.809, 40.843, 40.913, 40.971, 41.087, 41.145, 41.227, 41.343, 41.842]}, "text": "Then there is a problem with the most famous version of this theory. Ah-bel claimed that the philosopher Em-peddokleez reported finding such bones on Sicily. Later researchers have found no surviving ancient source that says so. The palaeontologist Mark Witton and Richard Hing put it bluntly: without that story, they argue, the idea becomes baseless. Elephant fossils and one-eyed monsters turn up all over the world, and no ancient text or artwork makes the Sicilian link stand out. The scholars of Greek myth Mercedes Ah-gheereh and Richard Buxton make a similar point. Theories like this sound plausible, they say, but lack a chain of argument linking origin to myth.", "orig": "Then there is a problem with the most famous version of this theory. Abel claimed that the philosopher Empedocles reported finding such bones on Sicily. Later researchers have found no surviving ancient source that says so. The palaeontologist Mark Witton and Richard Hing put it bluntly: without that story, they argue, the idea becomes baseless. Elephant fossils and one-eyed monsters turn up all over the world, and no ancient text or artwork makes the Sicilian link stand out. The scholars of Greek myth Mercedes Aguirre and Richard Buxton make a similar point. Theories like this sound plausible, they say, but lack a chain of argument linking origin to myth."}